The Security Council,
Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its relevant resolutions on the situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question, in particular resolutions 2712 (2023), 2720 (2023), 2728 (2024) and 2735 (2024),
Reaffirming the obligation to respect and ensure respect for international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality and precautions in attack, and the obligation to protect civilians,
Expressing grave concern at the catastrophic humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip, including the tragic loss of thousands of civilian lives, widespread displacement, destruction of civilian infrastructure, the collapse of health services and the risk of famine, and emphasizing the urgent need for full, rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all Palestinian civilians in need,
Condemning all attacks against civilians, including the Hamas-led attacks of 7 October 2023, and all acts of hostage-taking,
Gravely concerned by the continued violence and suffering in Gaza, the West Bank, including East Jerusalem, and the region, and recalling the obligations of all parties under international law,
Reiterating that the Gaza Strip constitutes an integral part of the territory occupied in 1967, and expressing grave concern over any attempt at demographic or territorial change, including any action that reduces the territory of Gaza, and reaffirming the importance of unifying the Gaza Strip with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority,
Reaffirming the vision of the two-State solution where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, consistent with international law and relevant United Nations resolutions,
Welcoming the diplomatic efforts of Egypt, Qatar, the United States of America and other Member States aimed at achieving a ceasefire, the release of hostages and a political process,
Stressing the urgent need for full implementation of resolution 2735 (2024) and the ceasefire proposal welcomed therein,
Deeply concerned by the escalating violence in the region, including in Lebanon, and calling for de-escalation and respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all States in the region,
1. Demands the full and immediate implementation of resolution 2735 (2024), including an immediate, full and complete ceasefire in Gaza, the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages, the return of the remains of deceased hostages, and the withdrawal of Israeli forces from populated areas in Gaza in accordance with the terms of the proposal, without delay and without conditions;
2. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law, including by ensuring the protection of civilians, humanitarian personnel, United Nations personnel, journalists and medical personnel, and by refraining from attacks on civilian objects, including hospitals, schools and United Nations facilities;
3. Demands that all parties facilitate the immediate, safe, unhindered and sustained delivery of humanitarian assistance at scale to all Palestinian civilians in need throughout the Gaza Strip, particularly in northern Gaza, including by opening all crossings, expediting aid deliveries, ensuring safe humanitarian corridors and lifting restrictions that impede humanitarian access, calls for full respect for the inviolability of United Nations premises and personnel and for support to United Nations humanitarian agencies, including UNRWA, to enable them to carry out their mandates, and calls upon Member States to support humanitarian efforts;
4. Rejects any forced displacement of the Palestinian civilian population, and demands that all parties respect the right of displaced Palestinians to return to their homes and neighborhoods in all areas of Gaza, including in the north;
5. Rejects any attempt at demographic or territorial change in the Gaza Strip, including any actions that reduce the territory of Gaza, and stresses the importance of unifying the Gaza Strip with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority;
6. Calls upon all parties to refrain from unilateral actions that undermine the viability of the two-State solution, including settlement activities, and reiterates that Israeli settlements in the occupied Palestinian territory, including East Jerusalem, are illegal under international law;
7. Reiterates the need for accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and international human rights law, and stresses the importance of independent and impartial investigations;
8. Emphasizes the urgent need to revitalize diplomatic efforts towards a comprehensive, just and lasting peace, and calls for the resumption of credible negotiations on all final status issues, in accordance with international law and relevant United Nations resolutions;
9. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 30 days on the implementation of this resolution;
10. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its relevant resolutions on the situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question, in particular resolutions 2712 (2023), 2720 (2023), 2728 (2024) and 2735 (2024),
