Condemning in the strongest terms all attacks against civilians, including the Hamas-led attacks of 7 October 2023, and all acts of hostage-taking, including sexual and gender-based violence, and stressing that all acts of terrorism are criminal and unjustifiable,
Condemning also all indiscriminate attacks against civilians and civilian infrastructure in Gaza and the region, and recalling the obligations of all parties under international law,
Reaffirming the inherent right of all States to self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and international law,
Gravely concerned by the continued violence and suffering in Gaza, the West Bank, including East Jerusalem, and the region,
Reiterating that the Gaza Strip constitutes an integral part of the territory occupied in 1967, and expressing grave concern over any attempt at demographic or territorial change, including any action that reduces the territory of Gaza, and reaffirming the importance of unifying the Gaza Strip with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority,
Reaffirming the vision of the two-State solution where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders based on the 1967 borders, consistent with international law and relevant United Nations resolutions,
Welcoming the diplomatic efforts of Egypt, Qatar, the United States of America and other Member States aimed at achieving a ceasefire, the release of hostages and a political process,
Welcoming the acceptance by Israel of the ceasefire proposal announced on 31 May 2024, calling upon Hamas to accept and implement it without delay and without conditions, and stressing the urgent need for full implementation of resolution 2735 (2024) and the ceasefire proposal welcomed therein,
Deeply concerned by the escalating violence in the region, including in Lebanon, and calling for de-escalation and respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of all States in the region,
1. Demands an immediate, unconditional, lasting and permanent ceasefire in Gaza, to be respected by all parties, and to that end demands the full and immediate implementation of resolution 2735 (2024), including the ceasefire proposal announced on 31 May 2024; demands that Hamas accept and implement the proposal without delay and without conditions; and further demands that the immediate, full and complete ceasefire in Gaza be coupled with the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages, the return of the remains of deceased hostages, the release of all Palestinian detainees, including those arbitrarily or illegally detained, and the withdrawal of Israeli forces from populated areas in Gaza, in accordance with the terms of the proposal and resolution 2735 (2024), without delay and without conditions;
2. Demands that all parties comply with their obligations under international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including by ensuring the protection of civilians, humanitarian personnel, United Nations personnel, journalists and medical personnel, and by refraining from attacks on civilian objects, including hospitals, schools and United Nations facilities;
3. Demands full compliance with the binding provisional measures ordered by the International Court of Justice on 26 January 2024, 28 March 2024 and 24 May 2024 in the case concerning the Application of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide in the Gaza Strip; recalls that such orders are binding; and reaffirms the obligation to respect and ensure respect for international humanitarian law and international human rights law;
4. Demands that all parties facilitate the immediate, safe, unhindered and sustained delivery of humanitarian assistance at scale, in accordance with humanitarian principles, to all Palestinian civilians in need throughout the Gaza Strip, particularly in northern Gaza, including by opening all crossings, expediting aid deliveries, ensuring safe humanitarian corridors and lifting the blockade of Gaza and all restrictions that impede humanitarian access; calls for full respect for the inviolability of United Nations premises and personnel and for support to United Nations humanitarian agencies, including UNRWA, to enable them to carry out their mandates, consistent with their respective mandates and in accordance with international law; and calls upon Member States to support humanitarian efforts;
5. Rejects any forced displacement of the Palestinian civilian population, and demands that all parties respect the right of displaced Palestinians to return to their homes and neighborhoods in all areas of Gaza, including in the north;
6. Rejects any attempt at demographic or territorial change in the Gaza Strip, including any actions that reduce the territory of Gaza, and stresses the importance of unifying the Gaza Strip with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority;
7. Calls upon all parties to refrain from unilateral actions that undermine the viability of the two-State solution, including settlement activities; condemns all acts of violence against civilians in the occupied West Bank, including by Israeli settlers; and reiterates that Israeli settlements in the occupied Palestinian territory, including East Jerusalem, are illegal under international law and undermine the viability of the two-State solution;
8. Reiterates the need for accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and international human rights law, including for the Hamas-led attacks of 7 October 2023 and all acts of hostage-taking, and stresses the importance of independent and impartial investigations;
9. Emphasizes the urgent need to revitalize diplomatic efforts towards a comprehensive, just and lasting peace, and calls for the resumption of credible negotiations on all final status issues, in accordance with international law and relevant United Nations resolutions;
10. Condemns all attacks against civilians and civilian infrastructure in the region, including attacks by Hezbollah against Israel, and indiscriminate attacks by Israeli forces against the Palestinian civilian population in Gaza; reaffirms the inherent right of all States to self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and international law; demands an immediate cessation of hostilities in Lebanon, full respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Lebanon and Syria, and full implementation of resolution 1701 (2006); and calls for de-escalation across the region;
11. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council within 30 days on the implementation of this resolution;
12. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

